ple, my opponent has a bit of a wall at F4-F7, so I need to check whether to break it or how to use it. Playing the move carefully is the best strategy.\n\n**Final Decision**\n\nAfter the assessment, I've concluded that E3 is the strongest move. It builds a solid, central structure without exposing myself. It flips one piece and seems the most defensive (in a good way) and also gives me the most future options.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The move (E, 3) is a strong central move. It is a \'quiet\' move that flips only one opponent disc (E4), preserving the stability of the board and minimizing the number of new frontier discs available to the opponent. It strengthens the player\'s control of the center without conceding edge or corner access (unlike D2 or F3 which might open up edge moves for the opponent). C-squares like B2, G7, H2 are strategically poor at this stage.\n\nReason: (E, 3) is the best choice as it is a central move located in the "box" (rows 3-6, cols C-F). It flips only one disc, keeping the game tight and minimizing the opponent\'s counter-attack options. Other moves like D2 or F3 expose edge squares (row 1 or col H) to the opponent, while moves like B2, G7, or H2 occupy dangerous C-squares adjacent to corners, which is strategically disadvantageous in the opening/midgame.\n\nAnswer: (E, 3)'}
