ce on row 2 exposed to the empty row 1. It allows the opponent to play A3 (an edge move) by using the new piece at B3 as an anchor, which is strategically disadvantageous.\n- **(E, 2)**: This is a "quiet" internal move that flips only E3. It fills a gap and maintains a compact shape. It is a good candidate but passive.\n- **(F, 1)**: This is an edge move. It flips the opponent\'s piece at E1. Since you already hold D1, playing F1 creates a connected block of three discs (D1-E1-F1) on the left edge. This formation is stable because the opponent cannot easily flank it (squares C1 and G1 do not currently allow flipping this segment). Removing the opponent\'s presence on the edge restricts their mobility.\n- **(G, 4), (G, 5), (G, 6)**: These moves on row 7 are adjacent to the empty bottom edge, potentially giving the opponent access to edge squares or corners if not calculated perfectly.\n\nComparing the best candidates, (F, 1) is superior to (E, 2) because it actively secures territory on the edge and removes the opponent\'s potential anchor there, rather than just waiting in the center.\n\nReason: The move (F, 1) is the best choice as it secures a stable position on the left edge (Column 1). By flipping the opponent\'s piece at E1 and connecting with your existing piece at D1, you create a solid block of three pieces that is difficult for the opponent to attack. This move restricts the opponent\'s mobility on that side of the board and avoids the risks associated with playing near open corners (C-squares/X-squares) or giving up edge access.\n\nAnswer: (F, 1)'}
